  <!-- ============================ 2. INTRODUCTION ============================ -->
  <section class="slide">
    <div class="slide-kicker">Introduction</div>
    <h1>Why Forest Biomass, and Why RGB-only Is Hard</h1>
    <div class="slide-body">
      <div class="col">
        <ul class="points">
          <li>Forests store an estimated <strong>861 Pg C</strong> and form a net atmospheric sink of about <strong>2.4–3.5 Pg C yr⁻¹</strong>, the largest terrestrial carbon reservoir.</li>
          <li><strong>Above-ground biomass (AGB, t ha⁻¹)</strong> is the most variable and most directly observable part of forest carbon, and the variable shared by IPCC Tier-2/3 inventories, REDD+ reporting, and carbon credits.</li>
          <li>Yet landscape-to-global AGB products still disagree by <strong>20–50%</strong> in structurally heterogeneous or data-sparse regions.</li>
          <li>The most accurate sensors are constrained: <strong>LiDAR</strong> is reliable but expensive, licensed, and sample-only, while multispectral and SAR <em>saturate</em> at high biomass.</li>
          <li>In developing countries such as <strong>Pakistan</strong>, LiDAR is rarely available and national field inventories are small (n &lt; 150 plots).</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card good tight">
          <h3>Driving Question</h3>
          <p>Can AGB be estimated accurately from the <strong>most widely available imagery</strong>: standard high-resolution RGB, captured with no specialised spectral hardware?</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col">
        <figure class="figure">
          <img src="assets/images/Graphical_abstract.jpeg" alt="Graphical abstract of the RGB-only biomass pipeline">
          <figcaption>Graphical abstract. Frozen DINOv2 descriptors from RGB patches feed lightweight regressors to produce plot-level AGB and wall-to-wall carbon maps.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span class="cite">Pan et al. (2011, 2024); Avitabile et al. (2016); Harris et al. (2021); Araza et al. (2022); IPCC (2019)</span>
