  <!-- ============================ 5. RESEARCH GAPS ============================ -->
  <section class="slide">
    <div class="slide-kicker">Research Gaps</div>
    <h1>Gaps Identified in the Literature</h1>
    <div class="slide-body">
      <div class="col">
        <ul class="points">
          <li><strong>Gap 1 · Modality cost.</strong> Policy-grade accuracy usually demands LiDAR or multispectral sensors that are far more expensive and less available than RGB.</li>
          <li><strong>Gap 2 · Feature ceiling.</strong> RGB pipelines rely on hand-crafted GLCM and VI features that capture only low-order statistics and generalise poorly across sites.</li>
          <li><strong>Gap 3 · Untested transfer.</strong> Frozen SSL ViT descriptors are proven for canopy height (with LiDAR) and crop phenotyping, but not for plot-level forest AGB.</li>
          <li><strong>Gap 4 · No cross-ecosystem evidence.</strong> No study evaluates one frozen RGB-only pipeline across geographically distinct forests.</li>
          <li><strong>Gap 5 · Small samples.</strong> National inventories have n &lt; 150 plots, so high-dimensional features overfit without regularisation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="col narrow">
        <div class="card warn">
          <h3>The Unfilled Space</h3>
          <p>No prior work tests whether <strong>frozen self-supervised ViT descriptors alone</strong>, with no fine-tuning and no multispectral, SAR, or LiDAR, can support plot-level forest AGB regression across heterogeneous ecosystems using only RGB.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card good tight">
          <h3>This Thesis</h3>
          <p>Sits at the intersection of the <strong>most accessible sensor</strong> (RGB) and the <strong>most modern features</strong> (frozen DINOv2).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
