  <!-- ============================ 9. METHODOLOGY (pipeline) ============================ -->
  <section class="slide">
    <div class="slide-kicker">Methodology</div>
    <h1>A Five-Stage RGB-Only Pipeline</h1>
    <div class="slide-body">
      <div class="col narrow">
        <ul class="points">
          <li><strong>1 · Patch extraction.</strong> Crop a plot-centred RGB patch, resize to 224×224, and normalise with ImageNet statistics.</li>
          <li><strong>2 · Descriptor extraction.</strong> Pass the patch through a frozen <strong>DINOv2 ViT-Small</strong> to obtain a 384-D CLS-token descriptor. No fine-tuning.</li>
          <li><strong>3 · Descriptor selection.</strong> Rank dimensions by |Pearson r| with AGB on the training set only, then keep the top N.</li>
          <li><strong>4 · Unsupervised grouping.</strong> Embed with UMAP and cluster with K-means, fitting cluster-specific models where the data support it.</li>
          <li><strong>5 · Regression.</strong> Apply NNLS, ridge, or XGBoost to predict plot-level AGB and wall-to-wall maps.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card tight">
          <h3>Key Principle</h3>
          <p>The encoder is frozen, so the same backbone serves both sites and only the lightweight regressor is fitted per site.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col wide">
        <figure class="figure">
          <img src="assets/images/figure2.jpeg" alt="Five-stage methodological pipeline diagram">
          <figcaption><b>Fig. 2.</b> The methodological pipeline. An RGB patch is encoded by a frozen DINOv2 ViT-Small into a 384-D descriptor, descriptors are ranked by correlation with AGB and grouped with UMAP and K-means, and NNLS, ridge, or XGBoost produce plot-level AGB and wall-to-wall biomass maps.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span class="cite">Materials &amp; Methods §2.2 · Oquab et al. (2024) DINOv2; Dosovitskiy et al. (2021) ViT</span>
      <span class="brand">NUST · SEECS</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 9b. NNLS METHOD ============================ -->
  <section class="slide">
    <div class="slide-kicker">Methodology · Primary Regressor</div>
    <h1>Non-Negative Least Squares (NNLS)</h1>
    <div class="slide-body">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:1.0rem;">An <strong>instance-based</strong> regressor. For a query patch it selects the <strong>k nearest training plots</strong> in descriptor space, reconstructs the query descriptor as a non-negative simplex combination of those neighbours, then applies the same weights to their measured AGB.</p>
        <div class="eq-block">
          <div class="eq-label">Step 1 · Reconstruct the query under a simplex constraint</div>
          <div class="eq">min<sub>w&nbsp;∈&nbsp;ℝᵏ</sub> ‖ A w − b ‖₂² &nbsp;<span class="small">subject to</span>&nbsp; w ≥ 0,&nbsp; 1ᵀw = 1</div>
          <div class="eq-label">Step 2 · Predict AGB from the neighbour labels</div>
          <div class="eq">ŷ = Σ<sub>i=1</sub><sup>k</sup> wᵢ yᵢ</div>
        </div>
        <ul class="points" style="margin-top:0.2rem;">
          <li><strong>A</strong> ∈ ℝ<sup>d×k</sup> holds the k neighbour descriptors as columns, <strong>b</strong> ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup> is the query descriptor, <strong>w</strong> are the weights, and <strong>y<sub>i</sub></strong> is the AGB of neighbour i.</li>
          <li>Solved with the active-set algorithm for non-negativity-constrained least squares (Lawson &amp; Hanson 1974; Bro &amp; De Jong 1997).</li>
          <li>The simplex constraint <strong>w ≥ 0, 1ᵀw = 1</strong> makes each prediction a convex combination of training AGB, so it cannot extrapolate beyond the observed range or return a negative biomass.</li>
          <li>The only hyperparameters are the <strong>neighbourhood size k</strong> and the <strong>distance metric</strong> (Euclidean or cosine), both selected by cross-validation inside each training fold.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="col narrow">
        <div class="card good" style="margin-top:0;">
          <h3>Why NNLS for n &lt; 150</h3>
          <ul class="points" style="margin-top:0.2rem;">
            <li><strong>Small-sample robust.</strong> No global parametric fit, so it avoids the overfitting XGBoost shows at n = 25.</li>
            <li><strong>Physically grounded.</strong> Non-negative weights match non-negative biomass, with no saturation at high AGB.</li>
            <li><strong>Interpretable.</strong> The weights form an explicit mixture over reference plots.</li>
            <li><strong>Smooth maps.</strong> Convex blending yields spatially coherent wall-to-wall rasters.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card tight">
          <h3>Lowest Error at Both Sites</h3>
          <p>DINOv2 + NNLS leads at <strong>%RMSE = 21.81</strong> (Balakot, K = 90, R² = 0.939) and <strong>13.85</strong> (Karlsruhe, K = 20, R² = 0.897), ahead of ridge and XGBoost.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span class="cite">Methodology §3.7.1, Eq. (NNLS) · Lawson &amp; Hanson (1974); Bro &amp; De Jong (1997)</span>
      <span class="brand">NUST · SEECS</span>
    </div>
  </section>

